= Chapter 3: Civil Liberties

Civil Liberties are a cluster of human rights that create space for civil society and put a limit on state totalitarianism. The best way to expound this is by beginning with the concept of totalitarianism. This well chosen word describes a state that is "total": there is a perfect overlap between the state and society: media is state media, education is state education, religion is state religion, and all opinions that the state is unwilling to hear are not to be heard. To have a non-totalitarian state means to recognize a space (hopefully a big space) for civil association outside the bounds of what the state may prefer. Citizens have their media, their opinions, their religion, their associations and clubs, set apart from the whims of the state, and the state must respect them.

It is not possible to study modern human rights without noticing the centrality of proportionality analysis. This is especialy true in the field of Civil Liberties. In the modern liberal West, the limits of my freedom of expression, of religion, of my privacy, etc. are always defined by reference to the rights and interests of others, and not to some overarching value system.

The main section of most judgments on Civil Liberties (but also elsewhere) is assessing whether a particular rights restrictive measure (1) follows a legitimate aim, (2) is provided by law, (3) is proportional and necessary in a democratic society. Steps 1 and 2 are almost always regarded as satisfied. Most goals adduced by the state are regarded as legitimate, and for a measure being provided by law, it does not need to have the backing of a parliamentary statute, some for of general regulation laid for in advance suffices. This means that all the assessment depends on step 3. Moreover, what counts as necessary is today understood as a dimension of proportionality: if it was completely unnecessary then it was disproportionate. So then all that is left is to analyze proportionality. Roughly: did the benefits obtained by the measure offset the cost of the rights restricted or lost.

One must concede that proportionality analysis has some merit. It recognizes that just claiming rights is not enough. One's rights must be somehow balanced or harmonized with the rights and interests of others. That said, proportionality analysis also feels very subjective and unpredictable. What is proportional to one may not be proportional to others. And it will be very difficult to know ahead of time what the Court will regard proportional.

There is also an issue of optics. Proportionality is judged from the vantage point of the judges. Theoretically it should be assessed from the vantage point of a "democratic society", but this phrase has been rarely cashed out into something concrete. What is missing then is the vantage point of the victim, or at least, of the losing party. THis can be seen most clearly in the PETA vs. Germany case below. To put oneself into the shoes of a believer in animal rights is to see that the comparison they made, regarded as offensive and dehumanizing by some, was exactly the comparison that PETA members ideologically (and perhaps existentially) needed to make. From the perspective of PETA, the campaign is not gratuitous in comparing factory farming with the holocaust, it is eloquent. To state the point in a more tame way, would be to betray the intensity with which this particular group sees this aspect of our ethics and economy.

It has been intimated that one must rely on proportionality analysis in the liberal West, because the idea is to have a society that values individual freedom above everything else, and recognizing no higher value than freedom, it must regulate the excercise of freedom by reference to the freedom's of others. However this framing can be disingenous. The liberal West is perhaps not a unique meta-society, that is committed to nothing above its own neutrality. Arguably it has a heirarchal system of values just like very other society, but is not willing to accept this as such. That is one reading one may have of the Lindholm case. The reality is that health is one of the highest values of the liberal West, and while nominally it appears as one value amongst others, it will reliably trump cases of conscience that we are likely to find eccentric or unwarranted. Here again we have the problem of optics. What is proportional or not is seen in the eyes of an insufficiently characterized vantage point of the judiciary. This vantage point is bound to reflect not pure neutrality, but what is hegemonic in our cultures.

Navigating the world of Civil Liberties requires sensitivity and patience. Sensitivity to the idea that what appears to be the right balance between x and y is a reflection of our own biases and prejudices and not visible from the eyes of the underdog. Patience because the meaning and extent of civil liberties perhaps can only be ironed out through the passage of time.

